seg-3a63ea85ed08 = This is step { "1" } of roadmap item C7 (adapters and notebooks for flight-dynamics tools; <a1>pre-registration</a1>). The adapters and notebooks that load these files into the tools themselves are in <a2><c1/></a2>: Orekit, Basilisk and ANISE so far.

# text
seg-94058315dfe4 = Input

# text
# c1: Output type: GEOMETRIC cartesian states
# c2: Output format: 2
# c3: Output units: KM-S
# c4: $$SOE
# c5: $$EOE
# c6: Target body name: CAPSTONE (spacecraft) (-1176)
# c7: Center body name: Moon (301)
# c8: Reference frame : ICRF
seg-0ab733f1cae7 = A JPL Horizons vector table as Horizons writes it: <c1/>, <c2/> (position and velocity), <c3/>, a TDB time scale, and the CSV form between <c4/> and <c5/> (Julian date, calendar date, X, Y, Z, VX, VY, VZ). The object, the centre and their NAIF ids (<c6/>, <c7/>), the frame (<c8/>) and the request time come from the table's header. A table in other units or another time scale is refused, not converted.

# text
# c1: tests/fixtures/capstone_horizons/capstone_2023-03_moon_icrf.txt
seg-b8c04678578b = The mission thread's CAPSTONE table is in the repository: <c1/> ({ "4,321" } states, { "1" } to { "31" } March { "2023" }, every { "10" } minutes, Moon-centred, ICRF).

# text
seg-32b620b3574e = Use

# text
# c1: --out
# c2: .oem
# c3: .opm
# c4: .bsp
# c5: --index
# c6: --spk-type 13|9
# c7: --degree
# c8: --creation-date
# c9: --object-id
# c10: read_back
seg-2af97e119de8 = Options: <c1/> (default: the input path with <c2/>, <c3/> or <c4/>), <c5/> (OPM), <c6/> and <c7/> (SPK; odd for type { "13" }), <c8/> and <c9/> (OEM and OPM headers). The command writes the file and prints a JSON report: the table's object, centre, NAIF ids, frame, time scale, number of states and span, the file's size and SHA-256, and <c10/>.

# text
# c1: ephem_export
# c2: kshana::ephem_export
# c3: content
# c4: encoding
# c5: utf-8
# c6: base64
# c7: tests/fixtures/capstone_horizons/ephem_export.sha256
seg-47ec263aa6b5 = The same function is <c1/> in Python, in WebAssembly and as an MCP tool, and <c2/> in Rust. Python, WebAssembly and MCP return the file in the report (<c3/>, with <c4/> <c5/>, or <c6/> for the SPK). Every surface writes the same bytes; the SHA-256 of the three CAPSTONE files are pinned in <c7/> and checked through the command line, Python and MCP.

# text
seg-fd09b891db14 = The values are copied, not computed.

# text
# c1: 2023-03-01T00:10:00.0000
# c2: TIME_SYSTEM = TDB
# c3: REF_FRAME
# c4: ICRF
# c5: CENTER_NAME
# c6: MOON
# c7: OBJECT_ID
# c8: --object-id
# c9: CREATION_DATE
# c10: --creation-date
seg-b29b27f69ba3 = <b>OEM and OPM.</b> Each double is written in its shortest round-trip decimal form. A reader that parses the text as IEEE doubles gets back the bits the table gave. Epochs are the table's calendar epochs, copied as written (<c1/>), with <c2/>. <c3/> is the table's frame (<c4/>), <c5/> its centre (<c6/>), <c7/> the NAIF id unless <c8/> gives one, and <c9/> the request time in the table's header unless <c10/> gives one (never the wall clock, so the same table gives the same file).

# text
# c1: J2000
# c2: src/spk_writer.rs
seg-084d94d4c848 = <b>SPK.</b> One segment: target and centre from the NAIF ids, the states as stored doubles, the epochs as TDB seconds past J2000 computed exactly from the calendar labels. ICRF is frame code { "1" } (<c1/>), the code SPICE kernels of ICRF-referred ephemerides use; no rotation is applied. The kernel is laid out as the SPICE Toolkit's own writer lays it out (<a1><c2/></a1>).

# text
seg-7beac13e2dc5 = The read-back

# text
# c1: read_back
# c2: identical
seg-b429e1f98e1e = After writing, the engine reads the file back with its own readers and compares every state with the one written (<c1/>): <c2/> counts the states whose six values have the same bits, the largest position and velocity differences, and the epochs that read back differently. For the SPK it also evaluates the interpolated state at each node epoch.

# text
# c1: spkezr
seg-880bdc413a8f = On the CAPSTONE table: all { "4,321" } states read back identical from the OEM and the SPK, and the OPM's one state too; no epoch differs. The SPK's interpolated state at the nodes is within { "2.1e-11" } km and { "5.6e-15" } km/s of the stored state (rounding in the Hermite arithmetic). Read with the SPICE Toolkit (CSPICE N0067 through spiceypy), the SPK's stored records and epochs are the same bits, and <c1/> at the nodes is within { "1.5e-11" } km and { "5.0e-15" } km/s.

# text
seg-0857a94a948e = Limits

# text
seg-2bb1857094b8 = One object per file. One Horizons table in, one segment out.

# text
seg-47cebe953abc = Horizons vector tables only, in km and km/s on TDB. Other state tables are not read yet.

# text
# c1: INTERPOLATION
seg-101dad28de33 = The OEM carries no covariance and no interpolation hint (<c1/> is not written); the reading tool chooses its own interpolation.

# text
seg-2a434206ddeb = The frame is written as the table names it. ICRF is not rotated to EME2000 for tools that only accept EME2000; the C7 adapters state what each tool accepts.

